  "sourceType": "SYNTHESISED",
  "sourceNotes": "Cawl is described in Welsh texts from the fourteenth century and is the national dish of Wales. The word simply means broth or soup in Welsh. S. Minwel Tibbott, Welsh Fare: A Selection of Traditional Recipes (1976), National Museum of Wales: documents the two-day cawl tradition. The specific combination of lamb, leek, swede, and parsnip is the settled Welsh version, with leeks as the defining vegetable.",
